            <section class="guide-summary" id="short-answer">
                <p class="guide-kicker">The short answer</p>
                <p>WeekScale keeps its working database on your device. There is no account, no WeekScale-operated cloud, no analytics, and no advertising. On Android the app is not even granted internet permission; on iOS it makes no direct network requests. Your weight history is yours, and it stays put.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="what-offline-means">
                <h2>What offline really means</h2>
                <p>&ldquo;Offline&rdquo; is only meaningful when it is specific. For a weight tracker it should mean a handful of concrete behaviors:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>The app can do its core job without any server.</li>
                    <li>No account is required to use it.</li>
                    <li>No analytics, advertising, or AI service receives your data.</li>
                    <li>The network permissions and SDKs match the privacy promise.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>WeekScale is built to those four points. It is a local-first app, which is a stronger and more specific claim than simply saying it &ldquo;cares about privacy.&rdquo; The <a class="inline-link" href="/private-weight-tracker">local-first guide</a> explains the concept in depth.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="data-map">
                <h2>WeekScale's data map</h2>
                <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
                    <table class="comparison-table data-map">
                        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Data or action</th><th scope="col">Where it goes</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
